ing to do  it's also really important that the  use of that antibody is consistent and  so whatever you're validating it for and  what you're validating it to use it for  is pretty consistent and very  reproducible day to  day it's also important to keep in mind  that there  can in some circumstances be a  difference between a validated antibody  and a useful antibody  now i've i've got here a very uh  clear  clear looking illustration of what a  validated antibody may look like this is  an antibody against the target protein  hr on the far left and we see a very  nice clean single band  and then as we move from left to right  our western blocks are getting a little  bit uglier so this this cap one antibody  here in the middle we're seeing a very  clear and obvious cap one western blot  with a nice clean band but we're also  seeing some off target binding as well  and then as we move left to right we're  seeing this mll1 antibody western blot  where the plot looks a lot worse we're  seeing a significant amount of off  target binding but we're still able to  see this this band and so it becomes  really important to be able to define  what is validated what is useful is  there a difference in those two things  and what are the implications of those  potential differences  antibody validation also can have some  very unique challenges and some unique  circumstances that you've got to be able  to kind of work through and really  understand the context of the biology of  your target protein  so one of these particular challenges is  the molecular weight pitfall  and by that i mean  when you go into a western blot and  something where you can very easily  assess molecular weight there are  obviously a lot of resources a bit  available for you to be able to predict  the molecular weight of that protein  now when the band your antibody is  binding to migrates at a different  molecular weight you're left to answer  the question what is this antibody on  target here on the le