back return whatever it is you  know getting that product to work well  is ultimately the goal for both sides um  as the customer  you need to receive an antibody and be  prepared to optimize  the product for whatever it is that  you're doing oftentimes you're going to  receive a product that says hey we  recommend this in a range let's say from  a one to two thousand dilution of one to  ten thousand don't always assume that  one side or the other is going to work  best for you be prepared to go ahead and  validate that product make sure that  it's doing what you expect it to do  and then prepare for subsequent  purchases of that same product to behave  in a very similar way  and also going into it make sure that  you have a very clear understanding of  what it is that you need  don't buy a product for a western blot  when you don't need it to western blot  or for an ihc when you don't want it to  isc make sure that what you're  purchasing it for that's what it's  designed to do  obviously you want to make sure that the  data that is being generated by the  manufacturer supports the type of work  that you want it to do  check and see if there's citations where  other researchers have used that product  for what it is that you're trying to do  and if you continue to struggle or if  you just have some questions on the  front end don't hesitate to reach out to  tech support it's often times that these  these tech support individuals are  scientists who know really well what it  is that you're trying to do and can help  you sort of adapt and optimize your  conditions to better suit those those  particular products  um  but still this does present a very large  challenge and it's something that can be  very fluid within the antibody world  just kind of at large  in 2016 and 2017 a group of researchers  that ultimately became known as the  international working group for antibody  validation published a couple of  articles  where they were trying to  sort of structure and organize the  validation of antibodies and sort of  standardize this across  all these different types of products  and what they ultimately proposed was a  series of what they call pillars  that should sort of support the research  utilizing all these products and these  five pillars i've got listed over here  on the right  these pillars included what they call  orthogonal strategies independent  antibody strategies genetic strategies  recombinant expression strategies and  capture mass spec strategies  um  we will talk about each of these in  depth here in just a few slides but i  did want to kind of talk on a more 10  000 foot level before we moved into that  degree of depth  you'll notice sort of in the antibody  manufacturing community that we begin to  adapt some of this language  and you'll see  products and landing pages that are  starting to allude to and refer to and  sort of illustrate  how these pillars are being used we've  got a belthole fortis landing page here  on the left biotechni rnd cst in the  bottom right and these are all sort of  versions of this antibody pillar  validation system that really sort of  fits in well with what these these  companies do  [Music]  historically and then are now being  adapted to help better validate and  better inform the customers who are  actually purchasing these products  um  what's really interesting and really  kind of cool about these these pillars  is that they have a what i would  describe as an additive effect to a  certain degree so  any one of these pillars  by itself doesn't always have a ton of  value for example if you've got  a mass spec and you've got a western  blot and they look like they should  that's a good piece of data that by  itself you could kind of make some  arguments to poke some holes in that but  when you start building you know two or  three of these pillars into sort of an  overall validation strategy  the additive effects of multiple pillars  can really do a lot more to support the  validation of a given product  now to a point that's true when you  start getting them above three and you  start getting into like four and five  pillars it starts to become a little bit  redundant and value gain by performing  some of those other pillar validations  you're really not learning a lot more  than what you already knew  um  it's also true that not every data set  is going to contribute equally to your  validation process so for example cap  one here in the top left you're seeing a  very ubiquitous expression pattern  through all these different cell lines  you don't really know if that's on  target or off target it could very  easily be an off target protein that  just happens to express in every cell  line you don't really know you don't  really have any way to prove it  if you know that that kaplan antibody  has been validated that western blot  creates some extra value for you so this  validation process isn't so much for you  to learn that you can use it or i guess  the better way to say that would be  validation teaches you that you can use  something the application data and some  of these  how to use something  it's also important to know that if  someone is to validate an antibody that  doesn't necessarily make it useful for  your work it just makes it useful for  theirs and so  it's important to remember that a  manufacturer validation process should  not replace  your validation process in your lab in  your hands in your conditions in your  cell culture conditions there's a lot of  novelty and a lot of variables that can  really play into what's happening in  your lab that are going to be different  and independent and unique from how that  that product is validated by the  manufacturer  so  from the  bethel fortis perspective we've sort of  adapted a little bit of a different  approach to these pillars so you'll  notice that the the top four pillars  were actually the same but we modified  the capture mass spec pillar because we  don't have a lot of mass spec capacity  here at bethel furnace  so we replaced that capture mass spec  with what we call a complementary assay  and a biological strategies um and we're  going to talk through those along with  the other six uh starting with this next  level  so the first pillar that we're going to  be discussing is what we refer to as the  independent antibodies pillar  and this is basically defined as using  two or more independent antibodies to  generate similar results  and it's important to keep in mind that  these antibodies need to be raised  against different epitopes of that  target protein  now historically at bethel we've used  what we refer to as a reciprocal ip  where we do in fact use two different  antibodies that were raised against two  different epitopes we will do  immunoprecipitation pull downs with both  of those antibodies and then blot with  one of them  and in a situation like the one we have  in the bottom left hand corner here with  ahr  if both antibodies are able to pull down  the same target protein we consider that  to be a very strong argument for that  binding our target protein and it does  fulfill the independent antibodies  pillar as it was two different  antibodies being used to generate the  same result  over here on the right this is a gata3  stain of a  tissue and we'll notice that these are  cereal sections and these two antibodies  are staining an extraordinarily similar  pattern in in both slides and so you  would say here that that that that data  is the same  and that those two antibodies are  performing the same and are binding to  the same target protein thus fulfilling  this pillar  the next pillar is the complementary  assays pillar and this is the other half  of the reciprocal ip where we have two  or more antibody dependent techniques  that are generating similar results so  with the reciprocal ip you've got an  immune precipitation and the western  blot of that ip that are gener  generating similar results this can also  include other scenarios like the one  that i've got illustrated at the bottom  of this slide here  where we've got the same antibody  binding well in a western blot this is a  sox10 antibody binding is sox10 and an  skml28  cell line  and you can see on the right here this  is an icc image of antibody staining  skml28 cells in culture we're getting  very strong signal and strong specific  signal on both of these we would say  that this did  in fact fulfill the complementary assays  pillar  the third pillar that we're going to  discuss is the orthogonal pillar and  this is where we use what we would call  an antibody independent technique to  help verify antibody dependent methods  and so the way this typically works is  you'll pull something out of a mass spec  scan  or mrna expression data and then compare  that to what you're seeing in a western  blot ihc or icc and so in this  particular example we've got cd19 uh  mrna expression data from a database  called gene visible  and you see that i've got two boxes in  the bottom left hand corner these are  boxing in the cell lines for ragi b  cells and dotty b cells and you can see  on the right we are getting very strong  and specific binding to both of these  cell lines i've got those blocks in in  red as well  and this would allow us to sort of  fulfill this orthogonal pillar during  our validation process here  the fourth pillar is the biological  characteristics pillar and this is  going to be a little bit more specific  to the biology as it relates to your  target protein  and these are more widely applicable  where we've got some sort of  phospho-specific antibody or some sort  of  protein that is expressed under certain  biological conditions unlike other  biological conditions so here in the  bottom left  i've got the induction of  phosphorylation after addition of  hydroxyurea to a cell culture line  we've got a phosphocdk1 there in that  that top window and you can see a very  strong and clear correlation  um in the presence and absence of  hydroxyurea  on the right we've got a series of ips  where we have treated  cells with topocide  and that etobicide treatment induces  phosphorylation which we can detect with  our fossil specific cap one antibody  there in the in the top corner and we  are also seeing the presence of  total cap one you can see there in the  re-blot in the bottom of that of that  window and this  you know again being able to sort of  manipulate and use this biology to  create the presence or absence of signal  in a western blot  allows us to fulfill the biological  characteristics pillar  the fifth pillar is what we would  describe as the recombinant expression  strategy and this one's a little bit  unique in that  it doesn't stand alone particularly well  but it can have a very solid additive  effect to  the validation strategy for any given  target  on the left  we are over expressing uh mass a mass b  and c mass  in a series of cell lines and you'll  notice that the  what we're trying to show here is  specificity to show that our mfa  antibody is not binding to mass b or c  mass  and you can see here in the top window  that we are seeing very specific binding  in that it is unique to map a  this can also be really helpful in a  scenario where whatever protein it is  that you're looking for it simply  doesn't express well  and so you can recombinantly express  those cells and then tag those for  example on the right here with an  anti-v5 or you can tag it with a v5 tag  and then detect it with an anti-v5  antibody and we can see staining in an  icc here on the far right or even iping  after a pull down with an anti-v5  antibody here right next to it so being  able to kind of use those tags and some  over expressing proteins to help step  into situations where the protein is  important but it's really difficult for  us to find  circumstances where those proteins are  expressing at a sufficiently high level  for  detection natively  finally here in pillar six this is what  we would refer to as the genetic  strategy  and for all intents and purposes you're  going to know this is a knockout cell  line so basically here on the left we  have recombinantly removed ubqln4s  gene from a mda mb231 cell line and we  can see a very clear distinction between  wild type and knockout cells and got  very strong signal in the wild type that  signals abrogated in the knockouts and  we can also correlate that in an icc  experiment as well you see that over  here on the right where we're getting  pretty good binding and staining in an  icc of wild type mda mv231s  where over here on the right we've got  knockout cells where that signal has  been abrogated  so again it's really important to keep  in mind that the  a single one of these pillars as a  general rule is not going to serve to  say that this is a validated product in  and of itself now  of these six the knockout genetic  strategies pillar is probably the most  capable of standing on its own but  typically we're going to kind of combine  this with a few other types of studies  and create more of a critical mass of  data that is you know very difficult to  argue with for all intents and purposes  so um  prior to purchasing anything  it's really important to keep in mind  like what what do you need this antibody  to be and this kind of harkens back to  that slide that i showed earlier with  the kind of green red gradient do you  need something specific or are you okay  using something like this block here on  the right where  you can still see your your target  protein but it's just not going to look  as pretty for some people that's all you  need for others you really need to be  able to pin down something like this  this cap one blot here over on the left  where it's a nice clean sharp target  band nothing else on there  again keep in mind the question of how  is this antibody been validated by the  manufacturer and make sure that you have  a good feel for species specificity what  applications this thing has been  validated for and what cell alliances  has been validated for and finally use  your use your scientific training like  can you look through this data and are  you confident in this validation data or  do you look at this and you're concerned  about what it is that you're seeing  finally after your purchase make sure  that you're using the manufacturer  recommendations as a guideline for how  you're utilizing this product are you  building in some other positive controls  are you sticking to their validated  species are you sticking to their  validated applications  don't be afraid to optimize this product  find the conditions that work for you  do be aware especially with polyclonal  products that as these antibodies age or  as lots change make sure that you  revalidate subsequent lots make sure  that you know what the implications of  those lot changes are how it affects  your work and ultimately what you need  to do to adjust for that  and finally again don't be afraid to  reach out to us most of the time  us antibody manufacturers all of our  colleagues in this field we like talking  about these products we we take a lot of  pride in the manufacturing and testing  of these products and frankly they're  they're fun for us to talk about  especially with with people who are  passionate about this work what we're  doing  and really want to dig into and  understand  the science behind these  bodies so  um  that's all i've got i'm happy to take  any questions  if you've noticed some of the unique  branding bethel laboratories was  purchased by fortis life sciences and if  you need to know anything or have any  further follow-up questions  about our products or validation please  visit us at bella.com
 

